<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Managing Stress in Changing Times</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Breathing to Relax</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in group projects. Communication: Aid in negotiating group roles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can resolve misunderstandings in group discussions about wellness practices by rephrasing statements and using gestures to guide focus (e.g., demonstrates a calming hand gesture and says, "Mindfulness means focusing on the present moment to reduce stress.")
            Can assist in assigning roles in wellness projects, ensuring that each classmate understands their responsibilities through visuals or examples (e.g., holds up a visual of relaxation techniques, and asks, "Would you like to cover relaxation techniques?")
            Can clarify group goals for wellness projects by using bilingual support and local examples (e.g., provides an infographic in both Spanish and English and explains, "Our goal is to share ways to manage stress with the class, such as visiting Parque Natural Metropolitano for nature walks.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 45%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Wellness Word Association" activity. The teacher writes "Stress Management" on the board and asks students to quickly call out any words they associate with it (e.g., <i>breathing, relaxation, exercise, music, mindfulness</i>). Then, the teacher asks, "If someone didn't understand what 'mindfulness' means, how would you explain it to them clearly?" This sets the stage for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to rephrase a concept. Teacher says, "Imagine a classmate says, 'Forest bathing is just like going for a regular walk.' I would say, 'What he means is that forest bathing involves *connecting with nature* in a *mindful* way to reduce stress, not just exercising. It's about *practicing deep breathing* and sensory awareness outdoors, which is very *beneficial* for your *mind* and *body*.'" The teacher also models polite suggestions for roles: "Perhaps you *could* cover 'deep breathing techniques' for our blog, Maria?" (Pronunciation focus: Stress 'soothing' and 'mindfulness' clearly, highlighting the /uː/ sound in 'soothing' and reduced vowels in 'mindfulness').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to rephrase or clarify someone's statement in a group project?" (Expected answer: To avoid misunderstandings, ensure everyone is on the same page, improve focus.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When trying to assign roles, why should we use polite language and offer examples?" (Expected answer: To encourage participation, be respectful, and ensure clarity of responsibilities.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Managing Stress in Changing Times".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A projected "Group Project Scenario" text (approximately 150-200 words) describing a fictional 11th-grade group working on their "Wellness Blog" project. The scenario highlights two common mediation challenges: (1) a student misunderstanding "biohacking" and thinking it's just about diet, and (2) another student feeling overwhelmed and unclear about their role in covering "relaxation techniques." The text uses target vocabulary (<i>mindfulness, breathing, relaxation, biohack, wellness, stress, focus, practices, techniques</i>) and grammar (gerunds like "<i>practicing mindfulness</i>").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will comprehend how misunderstandings and unclear roles can hinder a project and identify specific instances where mediation is needed to clarify goals and aid in negotiation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main problem the group in the scenario is facing?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements about the scenario and mark them true or false (e.g., "One student believes 'biohack' only involves eating healthy." "The group has clearly defined roles for everyone.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match each student's name in the scenario with their specific confusion or challenge regarding the Wellness Blog project.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Resolving misunderstandings, aiding in negotiating group roles, clarifying goals using gerunds (<i>practicing, connecting, listening</i>), modals for advice (<i>should, could, might, how about</i>), and target vocabulary (<i>mindfulness, relaxation, breathing, techniques, beneficial, focus, manage stress</i>). Pragmatic: Giving advice politely, offering suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Rephrasing Statements (Cloze & Matching).</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with "Confused Statements" from the scenario (or similar misunderstandings related to wellness concepts). Students must complete "Clarified Statements" using a word bank of gerunds and target vocabulary. Example: "Confused: 'Meditation is just for sleeping.' Clarified: 'What they mean is that *practicing meditation* helps *calm* your mind and *improve focus*, which can lead to better *relaxation*, not just sleeping.'" (Pronunciation focus: Pay attention to the reduced vowel sound in 'beneficial' and 'mindfulness' as students speak.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Role Negotiation & Goal Clarification.</b> Students work in pairs. One student presents a "problem statement" (e.g., "I don't know what to do for my part of the blog on 'Breathing to Relax'"). The other student must offer advice or suggest a role using modals and polite language. Example: "You <i>could</i> create a short guide on 'deep breathing techniques.' Or <i>how about</i> researching local quiet spots like Parque Omar for *practicing meditation*?" Students also practice clarifying goals: "Our goal is *sharing effective ways to manage stress*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: Mediation Role-Plays.</b> Students get a card with a specific group project challenge (e.g., "Your classmate thinks 'stress relief music' means loud party music," or "Two classmates disagree on who should research 'forest bathing'"). In pairs, they role-play, with one student acting confused or reluctant, and the other actively mediating by rephrasing, clarifying, and aiding in role negotiation using visuals or gestures where appropriate.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Managing Stress in Changing Times".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Mediation for "Wellness Blog." Students work in their established "Wellness Blog" project groups. The teacher provides each group with 2-3 specific, complex "mediation challenges" related to their blog content or group dynamics (e.g., "A group member is confused about the difference between *mindfulness* and *meditation* and how to explain it for the blog," "Two members want to cover the same 'relaxation techniques,' causing a conflict," "The group is unclear on the overall goal: is it just informative or should it also be interactive?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and works together to resolve the given challenges.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must actively practice mediation strategies: rephrasing, clarifying, asking clarifying questions, offering polite suggestions for roles, using visuals (e.g., sketching a quick diagram, holding up an example of a relaxation technique), and incorporating bilingual support if beneficial for local examples (e.g., explaining a stress-reducing activity like visiting *Parque Natural Metropolitano*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should aim to reach a group consensus or a clear understanding of roles and content for their "Wellness Blog."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing mediation strategies and offering support where needed, encouraging the use of gerunds ("<i>Practicing mindfulness</i> is key") and modals ("You *should* explain...").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment. The teacher uses a checklist to observe each group's use of mediation strategies. Additionally, students will complete a short peer assessment on their group members' mediation efforts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents one "resolved challenge" or "clarified goal" from their mediation activity. They must explain *how* they reached clarity or assigned roles, demonstrating the mediation techniques they used (e.g., "Juan was confused about 'biohack,' so I rephrased it by saying, '<i>Biohacking</i> involves *optimizing* your *mind* and *body* through various *practices* to *improve* your overall *wellness*, not just diet.' We used this diagram to show it.") They must use at least one gerund and one modal for advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy of rephrasing/explanation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectiveness in resolving misunderstandings and/or negotiating roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of gestures, visuals, or examples to aid comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of gerunds (e.g., "<i>Practicing mindfulness</i>") and modals for advice (e.g., "You <i>should try</i>...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of long /uː/ and multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respectful and polite tone during mediation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"On a scale of 1-5, how effectively did I help my group understand a difficult concept or assign a role today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific mediation strategy (e.g., rephrasing, using an example, a gesture) did I use most successfully to clarify information for others?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How important is clear communication and mediation when working on a project like our Wellness Blog, especially with the changing times impacting stress management?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new strategy I learned today to help clarify information or assign roles effectively for future projects?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What mediation strategies were most effective in helping students resolve misunderstandings and negotiate roles in their Wellness Blog groups?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully clarify concepts using gerunds and assign roles using modals? Were the pronunciation points (long /uː/, reduced vowels) addressed adequately?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed to further support B1.2 learners in complex mediation tasks or to reinforce the specific vocabulary and grammar? (e.g., more structured sentence frames for rephrasing, additional practice with polite suggestions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Breathing to Relax" theme and the development of the Wellness Blog. The mediation skills practiced today are highly transferable and will be essential for any collaborative projects or discussions in future themes, emphasizing the continuous need for clear communication and conflict resolution.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>